WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:08.350
What you are about to see is DNA's most extraordinary secret: how a 

00:00:08.350 --> 00:00:10.860
simple code is turned into flesh and blood.

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:16.860
It begins with a bundle of factors assembling at the start of a gene.

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:23.600
A gene is simply a length of DNA instruction, stretching away to the left.

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:32.550
The assembled factors trigger the first phase of the process,

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:37.980
reading off the information that will be needed to make the protein.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:41.810
Everything is ready to roll,

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:42.540
3,

00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:43.050
2,

00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:44.180
1, go!

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:50.850
The blue molecule racing along the DNA is reading the gene.

00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:57.560
It's unzipping the double helix and copying one of the two strands.

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:07.020
The yellow chain snaking out of the top is a copy of the genetic message,

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:10.990
and it's made of a close chemical cousin of DNA, called RNA.

00:01:14.990 --> 00:01:18.350
The building blocks to make the RNA enter through an intake hole.

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:24.020
They are matched to the DNA, letter by letter, to copy the As, 

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:27.390
Cs, Ts and Gs of the gene.

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:32.990
The only difference is that in the RNA copy,

00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:37.590
the letter T is replaced with a closely related building block, known as U.

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:47.650
You are watching this process, called transcription, in real time.

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:51.350
It's happening right now in almost every cell in your body.

